VR headsets aren't all created equally  we here at GameSpot have tested out Alex  on the four biggest VR headsets valve  index HTC vive pro oculus rift and  oculus quest we're gonna break down the  pros and cons of each headset and give  you our recommendation on the best way  to experience half-life Alex  I played the entirety of half-life Alex  on the valve index for review and  everything worked perfectly fine but  that speaks more to having the proper  space and physical setup for the base  station sensors and room for your own  actions the index is arguably the best  most sophisticated headset so far with a  max refresh rate of 144 Hertz and a 130  degree fov the highest of mainstream vr  gaming headsets and its resolution is on  par with the high-end vive pro of course  you need the proper rig to get the most  out of it and my setup with a core i7  7700 K CPU and NVIDIA r-tx 20/80  graphics card was on point  however the headset isn't necessarily  the reason why you'd want to play  half-life Alex on the index  it's the controllers the index  controllers are the closest a pair of VR  controllers come to imitating the feel  of having virtual hands and that's  because they're padded straps  comfortably hug your hands allowing you  to loosen your grip entirely and still  have the controllers in place now the  reason why this matters for half-life  alex is because the game always accounts  for your in-game hands giving them more  agency and a higher level of  interactivity than most VR games objects  doors and some mechanisms react to your  hand motions without having to grip them  simulating the actions of actually  moving these things in real life you'll  be catching items and throwing grenades  a lot in the game as well and it feels  just so right to perform these actions  when you can just let go altogether and  open your hands in these moments v NX  controllers also have pressure-sensitive  sensors on the grip for each finger so  when it comes to grabbing things in the  game you simply close your hand as if  you're really grabbing stuff all the  other VR controllers have a second set  of triggers for your middle fingers that  typically work to grab objects even  something as small as Zen grenades  showcase the importance of pressure  based interactivity these react to how  tightly you grip and activate when  squeezing them and it's nice to give the  middle finger to a dead combine soldier  and have the controller's recognize the  gesture in true form granted the valve  index might be the most expensive vr  gaming set up right now and seemingly  the hardest to find  it's not absolutely necessary to go with  the index if you just want to experience  half-life Alex but I do believe it's the  best option  the HTC vive seems like the next logical  headset of choice as it was co-created  by valve and shares the same ecosystem  as the index I used the vive Pro the  best version of the vibe and the second  best headset on the market in terms of  display my PC uses an RT X 27 d with 16  gigabytes of RAM and an i7 6700 K CPU  and Alex looks and runs great on it I  did run into some issues where the game  starts to drop frames and hitch while  streaming but that is the only technical  hiccup I've had and the rest of the time  has been smooth like with the index you  have the option for both sit down and  room scale the extra freedom to walk  around an environment can add a lot to  your experience and I highly recommend  it if you have the space for it being  able to physically duck and jump is  surprisingly fun and can help make the  game feel even more immersive just make  sure you have someone to watch over you  otherwise you might hit your hands like  I did so oh I just smacked my TV I think  where the vibe falls short is in its  controllers being in one of the first  major VR controllers they feel quite  primitive compared to the competition  lacking the indexes advanced finger  tracking means you use the trigger  buttons to hold objects it actually  works pretty well but it doesn't have  that natural feeling of picking up an  object the way you get with the index  even when compared to the oculus touch  controller is the v controllers have a  more clunky design that causes them to  Clank into each other don't get me wrong  they'll get the job done but it always  feels like you're the younger sibling  that got stuck with the inferior  third-party controller thankfully  because valve worked on both the v and  index you can mix and match the hardware  by using the index controllers with the  v the index controllers work flawlessly  with the vibe and after switching I  couldn't go back if you already own a  vibe I highly recommend picking up the  valve index controllers if you can for a  better Alex experience  the oculus maybe one of the older  headsets on our list but I've got to say  my experience playing half-life Alex on  it has been fantastic  I actually didn't run into many issues  my first few hours playing I only ended  up having to break away so I wouldn't  spend the rest of my day getting lost  exploring every corner of city 17 the  touch controllers are also some of my  favorites for general VR gameplay so I  felt right at home right from the get-go  I was also running this all on a PC  using a 1080 TI 16 gigs of ram and an i7  processor I have to say everything ran  very well  I opened steamvr through the oculus  software just to be safe as I started  Alex up I end up playing for about 30  minutes until I noticed that my height  seems sort of off after playing around  for a bit I found suggestions on the  internet saying that I needed to change  the height adjust option to hybrid after  doing that everything was smooth sailing  I really only had one crash after an  hour and 20 minutes of playing and  nothing really happened after that  after a while I did come to appreciate  just how easy it was to transfer from  standing up playing to sitting down and  playing one thing that really made my  experience great were the old touch  controllers sure the index has full  finger tracking but in my mind I still  have trouble telling myself the flow let  go of the controllers to grab objects in  the game the touch controllers really  only track half of your fingers the  actual act of grabbing is done with the  ring finger alone which never let me  lose my grip this works out well when  throwing things that zombies are combine  soldiers especially since the space that  I play in is really small technically  too small to actually be allowed to play  in all in all I never really had issues  using the old touch controllers  everything from shooting throwing items  into barnacle tentacles and even gravity  gloving ammo across the room to myself  to catch all felt very good I would say  my experience with half-life Alex on my  old oculus setup was overall great  outside of having to adjust the height  setting at the very beginning I never  really ran into any issues I'm also  proud of myself for not hitting any  walls or monitors while playing but I  did get scared by one of our cats  jumping onto my back while avoiding  headcrabs so take that as you will the  other case I can't wait to jump back in  a c-17 as soon as I get the chance  on paper the oculus quest is the most  questionable headset on this list given  you have a VR Eddie PC the only way to  play Alex on a quest is with an oculus  link cable or a lengthy USB C 2 USB 3.0  cable once the headset is connected you  have to enable oculus link and in theory  your PC will take over the processing  power and your quest will essentially  become an oculus rift i have played  roughly six hours of half-life Alex on  my quest with a 1070 TI 16 gigabytes of  RAM and i-5 4690k and a third-party USB  C 2 USB 3.0 cable I ordered off Amazon  the early hours were a rollercoaster  ride and not the good kind most of the  issues I ran into early on were due to  the oculus link software itself which is  still in beta and my PC within the first  15 minutes my headset not the game  crashed four times the game was still  running on my PC but the quest would  lock up navigating the world fel jittery  and if I turn my head too quickly the  image wouldn't always be able to keep up  the headset lock ups continued until I  plugged my quest directly into the  motherboard this solved the headset  crashes but the game was still a bit  jittery especially whenever I first  launched it eventually the performance  would even out but everything still felt  slightly frame II especially in open  areas however now is a good time to  mention that the footage you see here  looked much better in the headset itself  so don't let it turn you off entirely  the other hurdle you will undoubtedly  face with the quest are the controller's  halflife alex was designed with finger  tracking in mind which means your range  of motion is limited with the oculus  touch controllers your pointer fingers  are controlled with the triggers your  thumb closes when it's on the control  stick and the rest of your fingers are  mapped to the hand triggers on the side  this isn't a huge issue once you get the  hang of things but snatching objects  from afar with the gravity gloves can  feel a bit awkward at first since you  need to open your hand in order to  tether to the object that said once the  performance evened out and I got  acclimated to the headset and the oculus  touch controls half life Alex felt  playable sure it isn't as smooth as the  index or vive pro but it will certainly  get the job done if the quest is your  only option we recommend picking up an  official oculus link cable or since it's  near impossible to get the oculus  recommended anchor cable  it's no surprise that the index is the  ideal platform to play Alex on  considering they're both made by valve  but it's also the most expensive and in  short supply at the moment of this video  the vibe is definitely the next best  option it's designed for steamvr which  makes setup with Alex easy and you can  swap out the subpar vive controllers  with the indexes to take advantage of  the finger tracking while you may lose  some of the more advanced features  playing on an oculus you're still gonna  have a good time with Alex  the only headset we have trouble fully  recommending is the oculus quest as the  oculus link software is still in beta  and can have connection issues but if  you already own a quest and are willing  to put up with its shortcomings it's  still completely possible to play Alex  on it we hope this video gave you a  little insight into what half-life alex  is like on every major VR headset if you  want to know what we thought through the  game check out Michael homes review and  thank you for watching  you

